\description{
Tests each feature of a normalized count table for differential
abundance with a classical test: Wilcoxon's rank-sum test
(\code{da_wilcoxon()}), Welch's t-test (\code{da_ttest()}), or a linear model
(\code{da_lm()}), with the p-values adjusted for testing every feature. Each
gives, feature by feature, the results of base R's \code{\link[stats:wilcox.test]{stats::wilcox.test()}},
\code{\link[stats:t.test]{stats::t.test()}} or \code{\link[stats:lm]{stats::lm()}}, computed in parallel C from a sparse
table without a dense copy.
}
\details{
\strong{The tests.} For each feature, the transformed values of the treatment
and reference samples are compared:
\itemize{
\item \code{da_wilcoxon()}: \code{wilcox.test(trt, ref)}, two-sided, with its defaults
as of R 4.6.0: when both sides have fewer than 50 values, the exact
p-value (\code{\link[stats:Wilcoxon]{stats::pwilcox()}} without ties; with ties, the exact
distribution of the rank sum given the ties, as the Streitberg-Rohmel
shift algorithm computes it, twice the smaller tail); otherwise the
normal approximation, with the variance corrected for ties and a
continuity correction. Before R 4.6.0, \code{wilcox.test()} used the normal
approximation whenever there were ties, so its p-values differ there.
Ties are equal values (with \code{norm = 'clr'}, all of a sample's zeros
are one value, and zeros tie across samples only with \code{'percent'} or
\code{'none'}). A feature whose values are all equal gets a p-value of 1.
\item \code{da_ttest()}: \code{t.test(trt, ref, var.equal = var_equal, conf.level = conf_level)}.
\item \code{da_lm()}: \code{lm(y ~ ...)} of the feature's values \code{y} on the design of
\code{formula}, every coefficient tested against 0 with its t statistic
(\code{summary.lm()}), with intervals as \code{\link[stats:confint]{stats::confint()}}. A factor with
several levels gets a test per level against the reference (as
ANCOM-BC), not an F-test of the factor as a whole. With \code{norm = 'rclr'}, each feature is fitted to the samples it was observed in, so
its degrees of freedom (and, if a level has none of them, its aliased
coefficients) are its own.
}

Where base R stops with an error, these give \code{NaN} statistics and
p-values (never rejected) for that feature: a side with no values (with
\code{'rclr'}) or a single value (Welch's t-test, with a warning when a whole
side of the contrast has one sample), or, for the t-test, a feature
whose values are constant ("data are essentially constant").

\strong{Normalization.} The tests compare relative abundances, not absolute
ones: with \code{'clr'}, relative to each sample's geometric mean, so that a
change in a few abundant features shifts every other feature a little
the other way; with \code{'percent'} or \code{'log'}, relative to each sample's
total. \code{\link[=ancombc]{ancombc()}}, \code{\link[=ancom]{ancom()}} and \code{\link[=dirmult_ttest]{dirmult_ttest()}} deal with that
differently. In the benchmarks of Wirbel et al. (2024) on realistic
simulations, the Wilcoxon test, t-test and linear models were among the
few methods to control false discoveries, and adjusting the linear model
for confounders (\code{formula = ~ group + confounder}) mitigated them; they
found the clr \emph{lowered} the Wilcoxon test's sensitivity, while log
relative abundances (\code{norm = 'log'}) raised the t-test's and the linear
model's. Pelto et al. (2025) found the Wilcoxon test on relative
abundances (\code{norm = 'percent'}) and the t-test among the most
replicable methods across 69 gut datasets. The default (\code{'clr'})
follows the compositional reading of the other DA methods; try the
others with \code{\link[=da_test]{da_test()}}.

\strong{Computation.} The table is transformed per sample in C, without a
dense copy, and each feature is tested in parallel; each feature's
result depends only on its own values, so results are identical for any
number of \code{cpus}. The p-values, from the statistics, are those of base R
to rounding (about \eqn{10^{-12}}); \code{da_lm()} decomposes the design once
(a pivoted Householder QR, as \code{lm()}), and again per feature only for
features with missing values.
}
\section{Pseudocount}{

The \code{pseudocount} parameter is only relevant when \code{norm = 'clr'}
or \code{norm = 'log'}.

Zeros are undefined in the centered log-ratio (CLR) transformation and
in the log of proportions. If \code{norm} is \code{'clr'} or
\code{'log'}, \code{pseudocount} is \code{NULL} (the default), and
zeros are detected, the function uses half the minimum non-zero value
(\code{min(x[x>0]) / 2}) and issues a warning.

To suppress the warning, provide an explicit value (e.g., \code{1}), or
replace the zeros first with \code{\link{replace_zeros}()}, whose
result needs no pseudocount.

\strong{Why this matters:} The choice of pseudocount is not neutral; it acts as
a weighting factor that can significantly distort downstream results,
especially for sparse datasets. See Gloor et al. (2017) and Kaul et al.
(2017) for open-access discussions on the mathematical implications, or
Costea et al. (2014) for the impact on community clustering.

See \code{\link{aitchison}} for references.
}

\section{Input Types}{


The \code{counts} parameter is designed to accept a simple numeric matrix, but
seamlessly supports objects from the following biological data packages:
\itemize{
\item \code{phyloseq}
\item \code{rbiom}
\item \code{SummarizedExperiment}
\item \code{TreeSummarizedExperiment}
}

For large datasets, standard matrix operations may be slow. See
\code{vignette('performance')} for details on using optimized formats
(e.g. sparse matrices) and parallel processing.
}

\examples{
    # b1 and b2 are less abundant under treatment.
    x <- rbind(
      c( 20, 110, 100, 101, 100, 103, 104),
      c( 33, 110, 120, 100, 101, 100, 102),
      c( 12, 110, 100, 110, 100,  50,  90),
      c(202, 201,   9,  10,  10,  11,  11),
      c(200, 202,  10,  10,  13,  10,  10),
      c(203, 201,  14,  10,  10,  13,  12) )
    colnames(x) <- paste0('b', 1:7)
    grp <- rep(c('treatment', 'placebo'), each = 3)

    da_ttest(x, grp, contrast = c('treatment', 'placebo'))

    # With relative abundances (TSS), as the benchmarks.
    da_wilcoxon(x, grp, norm = 'percent', p_adjust = 'BH')

    # A linear model, adjusted for a covariate.
    meta <- data.frame(trt = grp, age = c(30, 41, 52, 33, 45, 50))
    res <- da_lm(x, ~ trt + age, meta, groups = 'trt')
    res
    plot(res)

}
